    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A diagram can also be used to divide polynomials even when a factor is not linear. Suppose we know \\((x^2-2x+5)\\) is a factor of \\(x^4+x^3-5x^2+23x-20\\). We could write \\((x^4+x^3-5x^2+23x-20)=(x^2-2x+5)(?x^2+?x+?)\\). Make a diagram and…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What are some things that could be true about the polynomial function defined by \\(p(x) = x^3 -5x^2 - 2x + 24\\) if we know \\(p(\\text-2)=0\\)? If we think about the graph of the polynomial, the point \\((\\text-2,0)\\) must be on the graph as a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Well, if we assume \\((x+2)\\) is a factor, there is some other polynomial \\(q(x)=ax^2+bx+c\\) where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are real numbers and \\(p(x)=(x+2)q(x)\\). (Can you see why \\(q(x)\\) has to have a degree of 2?) In the past, we have…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "One way to organize our thinking is to use a diagram. We first fill in \\((x+2)\\) and the leading term of \\(p(x)\\), \\(x^3\\). From this start, we see the leading term of \\(q(x)\\) must be \\(x^2\\), meaning \\(a=1\\), since \\(x \\boldcdot x^2 =…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "We then fill in the rest of the diagram using similar thinking and paying close attention to the signs of each term. For example, we put in a \\(2x^2\\) in the bottom left cell because that’s the product of \\(2\\) and \\(x^2\\). But that means…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Collecting all the terms in the interior of the diagram, we see that \\(x^3-5x^2-2x+24=(x+2)(x^2-7x+12)\\), so \\(q(x) = x^2-7x+12\\). Notice that the 24 in the bottom right was exactly what we needed, and it’s how we know that \\((x+2)\\) is a…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How the outputs of a function change as we look at input values further and further from 0.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This function shows different end behavior in the positive and negative directions. In the positive direction the values get larger and larger. In the negative direction the values get closer and closer to -3.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L12-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "12.3: More Factoring with Diagrams",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The power to which a factor occurs in the factored form of a polynomial. For example, in the polynomial \\((x-1)^2(x+3)\\), the factor \\(x-1\\) has multiplicity 2 and the factor \\(x+3\\) has multiplicity 1.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l12.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
